WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:09.160
In 1266, the monks of Sainte Genevieve monastery
in France sentenced a criminal to death for

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:15.610
the unimaginably horrific crime of murdering
a child and eating the body.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.930
It seems to have been a cut and dried case.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:23.840
As far as we can tell the condemned didn’t
dispute his sentence, although that might

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.080
be because he was a pig.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:34.560
And no, I don’t mean a nasty guy, I mean
an actual pig - what a swine……..

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:42.010
In a similar case in 1474, a rooster in the
Swiss city of Basel was tried, convicted and

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:48.449
executed "for the heinous and unnatural crime
of laying an egg."

00:00:48.449 --> 00:00:54.059
The event was clearly the work of Satan and,
as everyone knows, the eggs of Satanic roosters

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:59.379
can contain a cockatrice - a dragon-like creature
with two legs.

00:00:59.379 --> 00:01:02.859
Not something you want running around the
breakfast table.

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:07.909
In the end, the bird was burned alive and
the body discarded, which was probably a good

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:08.909
idea.

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:13.220
If there’s one thing I’ve learned in this
life, it’s that you should never eat Satan’s

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.560
cock, no matter how tasty it might look.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.689
These cases of capital punishment for animals
may seem bizarre, but they are not historically

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:23.689
unusual.

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:29.310
Records going back to the 9th century reveal
a long trend of humans putting animals on

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.979
trial for bad behaviour.

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:38.920
Culprits have included insects, slugs and
snails, leeches, weevils, small mammals like

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:46.689
mice and rats, big mammals like pigs and cows,
birds, worms, and a monkey suspected of being

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:48.439
a French spy.

00:01:48.439 --> 00:01:53.060
The number of these cases has gradually declined
over the centuries, but it hasn’t fallen

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.240
to zero.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:01.299
In 1916 in Tennessee, a circus elephant named
Mary was lynched for killing her handler,

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:08.160
an untrained homeless man who, until 24 hours
before, had never even seen an elephant.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:16.849
In 2004, in Kazakhstan, a female brown bear
named Katya was sent to an actual human prison

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:20.069
for 15 years after mauling two people.

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:27.690
And, as recently as 2008, a genuine court
of law in Macedonia convicted a bear for stealing

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.650
a beekeeper’s honey.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:34.980
The case dragged on for a year while the beekeeper
explained the trouble he had gone through

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:40.819
to frighten the bear off with disco lights
and high-tempo Serbian folk music.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:45.410
The bear, who clearly preferred drum and bass,
didn’t even have the courtesy to show up

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:50.489
for the court proceedings but managed to avoid
punishment on grounds of being a protected

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:53.239
species.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:58.580
To most people, the idea of sending an animal
to prison or sentencing it to be burned at

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:03.900
the stake, even just putting it on trial,
seems ridiculous, but these stories actually

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:10.099
raise some tricky questions about how responsible
we humans are for our own actions.

00:03:10.099 --> 00:03:16.550
You see, the reason animals across the vast
majority of the civilised world are not routinely

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:23.980
asked to plead guilty or not guilty to any
crimes they may have committed is, quite simply,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.000
because they can’t - and not just because
they don’t have the vocal cords to form

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.660
the words.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:36.000
Most societies correctly consider animals
to be unable to make moral decisions the same

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.579
way we do.

00:03:37.579 --> 00:03:44.000
The established thinking is that animals,
even well-trained ones, are ultimately slaves

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.769
to their instincts, acting out their urges
without first passing those impulses through

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:51.939
a filter of morality.

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:57.930
Humans, on the other hand, are believed to
be independent, free-thinking creatures capable

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.409
of making good moral judgements.

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:08.269
Based on this belief, when we make bad moral
decisions we usually risk some form of punishment

00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:11.739
at the hands of whoever’s in charge.

00:04:11.739 --> 00:04:17.799
This view of the world relies on the assumption
that each of us has free will, which means

00:04:17.799 --> 00:04:23.060
we have the freedom to choose our actions,
even if those actions result in us sharing

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:26.960
a prison cell with a large man named Nancy.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.750
But what if this view is wrong?

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:38.550
What if we actually have no more free will
than bears or pigs or weevils?

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:44.460
This question has been bugging philosophers
and legal theorists, but probably not bears,

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.840
for thousands of years and many regard it
as unsolvable.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:55.990
Over the last few decades, neuroscience and
quantum physics have chipped in with some

00:04:55.990 --> 00:05:04.490
useful insights, but the debate still rages:
do humans truly have free will and, if they

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:09.280
don’t, what does that mean for how we operate
society?

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:13.710
If you do something morally wrong, is it completely
your fault?

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:18.931
How about if you do something amazing, do
you really deserve all the credit?

00:05:18.931 --> 00:05:23.490
You may be watching this, thinking, “What
ridiculous questions, of course I have control

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:27.889
over my actions - how else could I be watching
Youtube in my underwear?”

00:05:27.889 --> 00:05:32.770
And that makes sense to all of us, mainly
because it just feels right - the notion of

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:36.070
free will, not your underwear.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:41.830
Every day we can point to numerous decisions
seemingly made at our own discretion.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:44.360
I was hungry, I ate a sandwich.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:46.789
I was tired, I went to sleep.

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:50.889
I was lonely, I remembered to obey the restraining
order.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:51.889
Simple.

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:58.949
Well, not if you agree with causal determinism,
the belief that every event is fully decided

00:05:58.949 --> 00:06:01.949
by some cause that came before.

00:06:01.949 --> 00:06:08.400
That cause was caused by something, which
in turn was caused by something else, and

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.699
so on.

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:17.590
In other words, there are no options or choices,
there’s just a never ending chain of cause-and-effect

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:22.699
stretching all the way back to the origin
of the universe.

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:27.990
You think you chose to have a sandwich for
lunch, but that choice was determined by a

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:35.240
collection of factors outside your conscious
awareness that guaranteed your lunchtime BLT

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.400
was inevitable.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:43.320
According to determinism, the impression that
you had some independent role to play in how

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:48.520
those events unfolded is nothing more than
an illusion.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.840
At its core, this is an old idea.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:07:01.879
It’s the thinking behind the ancient Greek
myth of the Fates, or the Moirai - three goddesses

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:07.479
who decide the destinies of mortals when they
are born, weaving the individual threads of

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:11.470
their lives into a tapestry of all humankind.

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:18.080
Their power was so strong not even Zeus himself
could alter the course of history, and he

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.720
was basically their boss.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:28.190
Similar themes exist in Norse mythology, German
paganism, Roman mythology, even Shakespeare’s

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:29.190
Macbeth.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:35.389
In Chinese myth and legend, an invisible red
thread of fate connects the fingers of two

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:38.070
people who are born to be together.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.449
Isn’t that lovely?

00:07:40.449 --> 00:07:46.530
Most of the world’s great religions also
include a version of this view, in which God

00:07:46.530 --> 00:07:52.240
or another form of all-knowing, all-powerful
deity has decided - or at least knows in advance

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.400
- what is to happen across time.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.580
Fate continues to be a sticky idea even in
a secular world.

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:06.240
Many people who regard themselves as agnostic
or atheist have had at least one experience

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.659
which seemed too amazing to have happened
by chance - like it was just - “meant to

00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:13.699
be.”

00:08:13.699 --> 00:08:17.419
But fate and determinism are not the same
things.

00:08:17.419 --> 00:08:22.389
Strictly speaking, belief in destiny is a
type of determinism called fatalism, that

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:27.920
says all events are mapped out in advance,
usually by some form of higher power.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:34.159
The grand plan doesn’t need to follow any
laws of cause-and-effect, it just is, as if

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:41.260
someone once made a movie about life across
all time and then pressed play.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:46.450
In ancient Greece, around the 7th century
BC, the notion began to emerge that it was

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:54.200
fundamental laws of nature, not the gods,
that decided the events of our daily lives.

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.521
Philosophers Democritus and his mentor Leucippus
developed the idea that we are all made of

00:08:59.521 --> 00:09:07.330
atoms and the motions of these atoms is decided
purely by the natural laws of causation.

00:09:07.330 --> 00:09:12.420
In their view, the future was entirely determined
by the past.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:18.670
As our understanding of the natural world
and the laws that govern it expanded, determinism

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:24.510
became increasingly informed by the emerging
disciplines of science, and few people had

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:30.860
a bigger impact on the development of modern
determinism than Isaac Newton, the seventeenth-century

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:37.240
mathematician, astronomer, physicist, theologian
and all around smart guy.

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:42.380
When Newton developed his laws of motion and
universal gravitation, he appeared to have

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:50.420
uncovered the very rules that govern the universe,
giving determinism a scientific foundation.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:57.170
With Newton’s principles it became, in theory,
possible to accurately predict the results

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.370
that would be caused by almost any action.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:06.460
In 1814, French scholar Pierre-Simon Laplace
- the first person to publish literature about

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:11.350
causal determinism - took this to the next
level with a thought experiment that became

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:14.300
known as Laplace’s Demon.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:21.770
Since classical physics explained every reaction
in existence, Laplace reasoned we could theoretically

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:27.600
understand the causes and effects that had
led us to any point in time.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.520
We could also predict the future from that
point in time onwards.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:39.770
So, if there were some intelligence, let’s
call it a demon or a god or giant turnip named

00:10:39.770 --> 00:10:46.230
Patrick, capable of knowing all the forces
operating in the universe and the exact location

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:52.680
of every particle in the universe, that intelligence
would be able to explain everything that had

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:58.230
happened in the past and predict everything
that would happen in the future.

00:10:58.230 --> 00:11:05.090
And there’s not a lot of space for free
will when every action is predictable and

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:07.080
already set in motion.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:13.580
The viability of Laplace’s Turnip has since
been undermined by the discovery of thermodynamic

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:19.832
irreversibility, among other things, but the
idea we’re ruled by cause and effect - and

00:11:19.832 --> 00:11:24.420
by extension the implication we might not
be as responsible for our own actions as we

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:28.570
think we are - hasn’t gone away.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:34.460
And in 1983, American Physiologist Benjamin
Libet conducted an experiment that rocked

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:41.900
the scientific world by providing compelling
evidence that we don’t have free will.

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:48.070
Libet was monitoring brain activity in individuals
carrying out a very simple action - tapping

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:50.490
their finger on a table.

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:56.200
His aim was to study how signals in our brain
turn into physical actions in the real world,

00:11:56.200 --> 00:12:04.810
but during the experiment he noticed something
strange: he was measuring brain activity before

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:11.240
his test subjects reported having consciously
decided to act - in other words their brains

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:16.400
were initiating finger movement before the
subject had consciously made the decision

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.320
to move.

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:24.040
As far as Libet was concerned, this was neurological
proof we are not the masters of our actions

00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:30.960
- our brains are making decisions before we’re
aware of them and somehow covering up the

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:32.040
evidence.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:37.240
We think we’re acting autonomously, but
it’s a trick of the mind.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:40.650
It was a bad day for free will.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:46.640
The results of Libet’s work became well-known,
for many ending the free will debate forever.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:52.040
But more recent research suggests the experiment
may have been flawed in the first place - that

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:56.640
the brain activity Libet was interpreting
as the initiator of finger movement in his

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:03.500
subjects might have been little more than
background brain activity muddying his results.

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:11.450
In 2012, neuroscientist Aaron Schurger reran
Libet’s experiments with a few tweaks accounting

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:17.400
for this possibility and found that our brains
might not be making sneaky decisions without

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:22.630
us knowing about them after all.The game was
back on for free will.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:28.650
Schurger’s research has definitely not proved
free will is a real thing, but it has forced

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:34.370
a rethink of determinism, at least the hard
kind.

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:39.390
Hard determinism is the type we’ve been
talking about until now - the belief that

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:45.990
free will and a deterministic universe are
incompatible - it’s one or the other.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:52.200
But soft determinism, usually referred to
as compatibilism, is the view that free will

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.910
and determinism can co-exist.

00:13:54.910 --> 00:14:01.130
For compatibilists, this allows us to avoid
simplifying the full complexity of human experience

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:06.920
down to a series of physical laws, a process
known as reductionism.

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:11.660
It also opens up space for the uncertainty
kicked up by quantum physics.

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:16.360
Hard determinism has its roots in the laws
of classical physics, but these rules don’t

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:21.440
apply at a quantum level, a rather inconvenient
issue that happens to be one of the biggest

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:24.170
unsolved problems in physics.

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:28.710
Scientists have yet to find a Theory of Everything
to explain the way the whole universe works,

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:34.310
from the super big to the sub-atomic, and
until then they have to accept that the quantum

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:39.940
world and the macro world mostly operate according
to different laws.

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:44.890
With this in mind, the closest thing we can
find to causal determinism at the quantum

00:14:44.890 --> 00:14:48.200
level are determined probabilities.

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:53.670
For example, it may not be possible to predict
the trajectory of a sub-atomic particle in

00:14:53.670 --> 00:15:00.100
the same way you can accurately predict the
trajectory of a planet or a frisbee, but particles

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:05.830
all obey determined probabilities that give
them a degree of predictability.

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:12.080
Some people, including Einstein, argue there
are hidden variables we simply can’t see

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:17.200
that would account for gaps in our ability
to predict quantum behaviour, and if we knew

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:27.330
these the universe would be fully deterministic,
but this is hotly debated by other physicists.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:32.170
Regardless of which side you take, quantum
physics currently casts reasonable doubt on

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:38.100
the idea of determinism - basically if the
world around us is inherently unpredictable,

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.910
true determinism can’t exist.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:46.640
Of course, the big question is not ‘Do you
have free will?’ but rather ‘Why should

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:47.750
you care?’

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:52.360
Sure, most of us like to think we have free
will because we prefer to feel like we make

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:59.490
our own choices, but if we are actually just
highly sophisticated robots acting out the

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:05.980
inevitable drama of a deterministic universe,
what difference does that really make?

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:11.140
We still feel like we make our own decisions
so everything ends up the same.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:17.700
Well, for one thing it could alter how forgiving
we are of each other’s behaviour.

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:24.080
Take for example the case of Charles Whitman,
a US marine known as the infamous Texas Tower

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:25.610
Sniper.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:30.991
On the 1st of August 1966, Whitman killed
his mother and wife, before heading to the

00:16:30.991 --> 00:16:38.360
University of Texas, where he proceeded to
kill another 14 people and wound 31 before

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:40.570
police shot him dead.

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:46.920
It remains one of the most horrific shootings
in American history, but a recent study questions

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:50.680
whether Whitman was truly responsible for
his actions.

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:55.340
Doctors performing the autopsy on Whitman’s
body found a brain tumour pressing against

00:16:55.340 --> 00:17:01.740
his amygdala, a part of the brain critical
to emotional regulation and behavioural control.

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:06.850
Whitman was one of 17 cases involved in a
study that looked at examples of criminal

00:17:06.850 --> 00:17:10.600
behaviour committed by people with brain lesions.

00:17:10.600 --> 00:17:14.500
Even though the research subjects had brain
lesions in different parts of their brains,

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:21.060
the lesions were all found in places connected
to a neurological system dubbed the ‘criminality-associated

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:22.260
network.’

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:26.250
This network is closely linked with brain
structures related to moral judgement and

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:32.890
decision-making, and researchers believe disruptions
to these brain systems could have been responsible

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:36.390
for the individuals’ criminal behaviour.

00:17:36.390 --> 00:17:41.570
This conclusion raises some difficult questions
about free will and how responsible any of

00:17:41.570 --> 00:17:43.920
us are for decisions.

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:49.600
If our actions can be influenced so significantly
by events in our brains, how much choice do

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:51.370
we really have in what we do?

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:56.100
Where do we draw the line of moral and legal
responsibility?

00:17:56.100 --> 00:18:11.830
In the end, is there really so much difference
between us and honey-stealing bears?

